  "text": "Question five. What is sessionization, and how do you do it in SQL? It groups one user's events into visits. A new session starts when the gap since their last event passes thirty minutes. User one has six hits in a day. Lag brings each row the timestamp before it. Flag a one where that gap is over thirty minutes, or where there's no earlier hit at all. Then a running sum of the flags is the session number. Gaps of ninety-five and two hundred five minutes start new sessions, so user one's six hits make three. Across all five users, twenty-four hits make twelve sessions. One more before the quick round: what counts as typical.\n\nQuestion six. How do you compute a median or a percentile in SQL? Here are twelve request times, made up for this video. Their average is four hundred seven milliseconds, slower than ten of the twelve requests. Percentile cont interpolates: the median is one sixty-five, halfway between the sixth and seventh values. Percentile disc returns a value that really occurred: one fifty. That's why latency is reported as a percentile. The ninetieth percentile is eight hundred thirty-six milliseconds: nine requests in ten finish within it, and the slow tail stays in view instead of being averaged away.",
